'''wheel-house''' (hwēl′hous), ''n.'' ''Naut.'', same as ''pilot-house''.

'''Wheelhouse’s operation for stricture.''' See ''operation''.

'''wheeling''' (hwē′ling), ''n.'' [Verbal n. of ''wheel''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.''] '''1.''' The act of traveling or of conveying a load on wheels, or in a wheeled vehicle.
{{smaller block|The sleighing is not as good as it was, and the state of the streets admits ''wheeling''. {{right|''Upper Ten Thousand'', ii.}}}}
'''2.''' Specifically, the art or practice of riding on a bicycle or a tricycle. [Colloq.]

'''Wheeling bridge case.''' See ''case''<sup>1</sup>.

'''wheel-jack''' (hwēl′jak), ''n.'' '''1.''' A lifting-jack having a projection to catch under the tire of a wheel.—'''2.''' An apparatus of which the lifting-bar is a cogged rack, worked by a pinion and hand-crank.

readings.c2s3:
